      {
        text: "There is no epistemic gap. Zombies are conceptually incoherent: a being that duplicates all functional roles thereby satisfies every requirement for consciousness, because 'consciousness' picks out a cluster of functional roles. Mary learns no new propositional fact — only a new ability (Lewis). The hard problem dissolves into the easy problems once we see that explaining the functions explains everything. Chalmers classifies this as type-A precisely because it closes the gap from the phenomenal side: the concept of consciousness is analyzable in functional terms.",
